WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.670
- Let me get my first guest on the line.

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:04.550
He's a man on a mission to
make sure we all stay informed

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:05.950
during this tough time.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.460
He's the anchor of NBC
Nightly News and Dateline.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.350
And recently, he added one more,

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.820
NBC Nightly News Kids Edition.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.317
All right, well Lester are you there?

00:00:16.317 --> 00:00:17.743
- I am, hey.

00:00:17.743 --> 00:00:19.070
- Hey, how are you?

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.320
Where are you zooming from?
- I'm doing okay.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.040
I mean, I'm trying to abide by the rules

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.910
and you know, I stand on my head

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:27.270
if they told me it
would flatten the curve.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.070
So, you know, other than walking the dog,

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.490
a couple of times a day and
make an occasional field runs,

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:34.510
I'm at home.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:35.430
- But where you are at right now,

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:36.280
is it your home?

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.230
It's beautiful.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.901
- Yeah, this is my home in lower Manhattan

00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:43.020
and fortunately we'd done some work

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.460
and we got in just in
time when all this hit.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.609
And you just can't
complain in times like this

00:00:47.609 --> 00:00:50.330
because a lot of folks can't, like us

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:51.692
who work at home.

00:00:51.692 --> 00:00:52.525
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:52.525 --> 00:00:54.560
- So, you know, we're
doing what we gotta do.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:55.393
- Yeah, no.

00:00:55.393 --> 00:00:56.280
Amen.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.610
And what's funny about your statement

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.640
is we have been living
in temporary housing,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.288
and let me know
(both laughing)

00:01:01.288 --> 00:01:02.641
it is not fun.

00:01:02.641 --> 00:01:03.474
- Yeah.

00:01:03.474 --> 00:01:05.930
- But we have a bit of an
end of the school year show

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.020
going on right now.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.383
What would you have said?

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.440
Or what would you have
been sad about I guess,

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.940
to miss at the end of your
high school experience?

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.240
- So much happens in that last month.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.890
I think, one of the cool things,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.100
I don't know if they do this anymore,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.410
but growing up in California,

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.900
Disneyland used to have grad nights.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.610
And they would close the park
from midnight to 5:00 a.m.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:30.290
And high school graduates from
all over the West would come

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.860
and just have a fun night.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.090
And I remember I really
looked forward to that

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.290
and I would hate to miss that

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.240
because it was like a celebration,

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.500
not only with your classmates,

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.310
but other 18 year olds who
were heading out into the world

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:43.400
in college.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.910
- So fun. (laughing)

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.500
I love Disney.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.333
All right,

00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:49.580
well you're doing the virtual
commencement speech this year

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.780
for Rutgers.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.680
- Yeah, Rutgers, they asked me
to be a commencement speaker

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.100
and you know, like a lot of schools,

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.020
they're not gonna have a
traditional commencement.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.040
So I'll be doing it in front of a camera.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.170
- I've often thought about this

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.080
cause you see a lot of
people in the limelight

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.357
giving commencement speeches and I'm like,

00:02:03.357 --> 00:02:05.070
"gosh, that's gotta be nerve-wracking!"

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.750
Right, like what are you gonna say?

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.500
- Yeah I've done
commencement speeches before.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.640
It's always awkward for me
cause I didn't finish college.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.740
So I always have to like
do that as my announcement,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.037
that, "Hey, you know, I never stood

00:02:16.037 --> 00:02:17.290
"where you guys are right now."

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.270
But it's a different message this year

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.010
because they're going
out into a job market,

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:23.730
that's essentially zilch right now,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.130
in terms of opportunities.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.697
I think my message to them it's gonna be,

00:02:27.697 --> 00:02:29.057
"this is your story to tell.

00:02:29.057 --> 00:02:32.397
"You are the generation that
is coming out of this thing.

00:02:32.397 --> 00:02:33.777
"You're gonna know how it ends

00:02:33.777 --> 00:02:36.300
"because we'll be playing
out this through years."

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.890
And I want them to think about that.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.103
Think about, you know, what
are we learning right now

00:02:41.103 --> 00:02:42.503
that we can apply?

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.670
I'm dying to know how this all ends

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:51.270
and how it changes in a world
where start looking at issues

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.100
like climate change differently now

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.910
because now we're seeing

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.830
the bad things can really be possible.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.667
Some of those things that
people may have warned us about

00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:00.680
but we never heard.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.720
Now we're hearing it

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.630
and now we get it that

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.854
all these people that toil away in labs

00:03:05.854 --> 00:03:09.010
and academic settings,
going over statistics

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:12.300
and possibilities, that
stuff can really happen.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.950
- But unless you're dealing with the virus

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.870
it's hard enough for grownups.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.770
Tell me about the newscast
you're doing just for kids.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.330
Like how's that going?

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.297
- Yeah, one of our senior producers say,

00:03:23.297 --> 00:03:24.130
"this is also scary.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.270
"Why don't we do a show aimed at kids?"

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:27.407
And immediately we're like,

00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:28.750
"yeah, that makes so sense."

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:29.583
I have to tell you,

00:03:29.583 --> 00:03:31.650
there were some nights
I'd be anchoring the news

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.520
and I'd be listening to the
words coming out of my mouth.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:34.817
I'm thinking, "Oh my goodness!

00:03:34.817 --> 00:03:35.907
"There are families watching,

00:03:35.907 --> 00:03:38.320
"there are children watching right now."

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.250
And there's no way to
really tell these stories

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.140
in a way that we can comfort
their particular needs.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.700
So this was a great opportunity.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.590
So, we don't shy away from the bad news.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:48.830
We acknowledge this is scary,

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.290
but we try and put it in some perspective

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:54.063
about ways that people are helping,

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.130
ways people are inspiring each other.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:03:59.870
Questions that kids may have about,

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.910
you know, can my pet get the
disease, those sorts of things.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.210
And so we allow the kids to
send in their video questions

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.800
and we try and deal
with it with our experts

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.250
and then have some fun moments.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.890
But, it's got over really
well, we're really proud of it.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:13.490
- Thank you so much for
keeping us all informed

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:14.323
for one thing.

00:04:14.323 --> 00:04:16.260
But is this the hardest thing
you've ever had to report on?

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.640
Cause I know you have so many jobs,

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:19.490
you've reported a lot,

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.380
but is this kind of really
hard and devastating?

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:22.525
- No,

00:04:22.525 --> 00:04:25.110
I mean it's certainly the
biggest story I've ever covered.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.160
I've covered up a lot of horrible stories

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.653
involving a lot of suffering.

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:33.590
The horrible earthquake
several years ago in Haiti

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.760
that had a staggering death toll.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.550
And I covered a famine in Somalia.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.680
So I've seen a lot of suffering.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.810
But this story has so many moving parts.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:44.770
I mean obviously it is health calamity,

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.870
but it's also this huge economic disaster

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:49.100
we're watching unfold.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.950
And you know, these are things
that both of those prongs

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:54.870
are not gonna be easy
to fix in a short term.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.470
And yes, we may get a vaccine,

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:59.090
we may get better treatments,
you know, in the medical side,

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:02.270
but still there's a lot
of people on this planet

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.100
who need those medicines and things.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.160
And in the same thing
on the economic side,

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.400
I mean the damage is mounting everyday.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.233
- Yes, yes.

00:05:09.233 --> 00:05:10.180
True.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.620
Was there one particular image or moment

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.920
when the seriousness of
it all really hit you?

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:16.760
Because I feel like it's
been a different moments

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:17.920
for everyone.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.820
- Yeah, I mean obviously
I deal in moments.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.583
But I think it was one day,

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:26.790
toward the last few days
that I was broadcasting

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.590
from a studio versus my home,

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.260
I was kind of holed up in my office.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.510
And I opened the door one day,
and I couldn't see anybody

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:34.343
and it was weird.

00:05:34.343 --> 00:05:38.510
I'm at 30 Rockefeller Plaza,
the Nightly News Newsroom

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:42.540
and we had so quickly move
people to work off-site.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:43.840
And it just hit me that one day

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.070
when I realized there's just
not a lot of people around here

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:47.570
right now.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.409
And I think that's when--
- Who's that?

00:05:49.409 --> 00:05:50.265
- This is weird.

00:05:50.265 --> 00:05:51.730
- Who's that?

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.544
- Oh, this is Lucy.

00:05:53.544 --> 00:05:54.377
- Hi, Lucy.

00:05:54.377 --> 00:05:55.210
Oh my gosh!

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:56.043
What kind of dog?

00:05:56.043 --> 00:05:58.270
I have Henry and Henry looks similar.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:02.320
- Oh, Lucy is a four-year-old
Australian Labradoodle.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:03.800
- Okay, I have a Goldendoodle.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:04.760
I was gonna say that.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.170
- She works with an Australian accent.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:07.070
It's very strange.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.070
I've always loved dogs.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.560
But during this period,

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.450
do you not find you're
hugging your dog more

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:16.740
than petting your dog more?

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.540
- My dog is in heaven
because I worked so much.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:20.373
And you need too.

00:06:20.373 --> 00:06:22.020
Cause I listed off all of your jobs.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.440
But like I'm around him so much now

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:25.273
and he can come with me,

00:06:25.273 --> 00:06:26.106
cause I'm on the ranch

00:06:26.106 --> 00:06:27.820
and he kind of comes and
goes in the all the time now.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:31.760
And he's gonna go into a depression

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:33.690
once we all go back to work. (laughing)

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:35.340
- Yeah, will.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:36.933
- And dogs are so healing.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:39.930
I don't know what it is
about their little spirits,

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:41.440
but like even just laying with them,

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.370
I've been in a bad mood.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:43.760
Just laying down on the floor with them,

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:45.260
just like cuddling with Henry.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.951
Because he's a big dog,
kinda like it looks Lucy,

00:06:47.951 --> 00:06:51.750
but he's a bigger dog and
it's just so comforting.

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:52.710
Right?

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:53.957
- It is comforting.

00:06:53.957 --> 00:06:56.557
I'm feeling, we were talking
about this just today that,

00:06:56.557 --> 00:06:57.390
"Oh my goodness!

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.967
"When I get back to work,
she's gonna be in depression."

00:06:59.967 --> 00:07:01.240
- She's not gonna get into depression

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.066
or they're all gonna throw
parties when we're gone.

00:07:03.066 --> 00:07:04.580
(laughing)
- Yeah, woo woo!

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:06.180
- Yeah, they're like, "get out."

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.220
So do you think that
there are any advantages

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:11.360
to doing the news from home?

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.458
Cause I feel like me doing the voice,

00:07:14.458 --> 00:07:17.120
and the talk show and
the recording record,

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.920
it is hard in this circumstance.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:19.770
But at the same time,

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.820
like right now I'm wearing
just like gym pants

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:23.157
and you can't tell.

00:07:23.157 --> 00:07:23.990
(laughing)

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:26.560
- Yeah, I mean there is comfort.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.290
I put on a suit and tie
and yes I wear pants.

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:32.880
But I felt like I hit rock bottom one day.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.130
I was doing the newscast,
middle of Nightly News

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:36.157
and I looked down, I realized,

00:07:36.157 --> 00:07:37.940
"Oh my goodness, I'm barefoot."

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:39.890
And it horrified me.

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:41.310
Obviously the audience couldn't see it,

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:43.710
but I'm like, "Oh my goodness,
this is where I'm at."

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:44.823
Hey, look at you.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:47.640
- I'm like, I took my shoes off.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.961
I was like, "well no one can see them."

00:07:49.961 --> 00:07:51.590
- I have my shoes on
for this interview now.

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:53.750
- But you do feel like you're like, wow,

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:55.900
they're really the bar,
there's no bar anymore.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:57.730
I've just gone so low. (laughing)

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:58.850
There is no bar.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:00.771
- No, but there's some advantages.

00:08:00.771 --> 00:08:02.140
I work alone.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:04.780
So, I mean I have to learn
how to flip some switches

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.060
and buttons and turn some lights on.

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:09.250
But I have a great bathroom access,

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:12.310
which, you know, big, long
news days is always good.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:13.800
So you know.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.480
- That's I know, I'm trying
to be positive about too.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:16.860
It is hard though cause I'm not like you,

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:17.920
I'm not a tech person.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.290
So I'm having to learn
how to do all this stuff

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:21.123
behind the scenes too.

00:08:21.123 --> 00:08:24.330
And you really start to go,
"thank you so much to my crew."

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:26.190
I already really appreciate my crew,

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:29.300
but like you really appreciate
your crew in this moment

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:30.160
and all that they do,

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:32.310
and the little things that kind of add up.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:34.410
- Yeah, I was doing a
newscast the other night,

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:37.210
and it was like 10 minutes before air,

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:39.080
and no one was talking to
me through my earpiece,

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.250
but I didn't think anything off it.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:41.810
Sometimes you're busy.

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:43.010
And then I hear the phone ring,

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:44.649
and then my wife comes
running and she goes,

00:08:44.649 --> 00:08:46.220
"your IFP, your IFP."

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.090
So somehow my communications
had cuts in the studio.

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:51.210
And so then it kicked off
a whole bunch of things

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:52.340
I had to dial back in.

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:55.810
But, she's my tech manager actually.

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:58.440
- That's what my husband's
been doing the same thing

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.120
and we have no idea what we're doing.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:00.953
So (laughing).

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:02.710
Yeah, it's different.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.060
Well, I see that, a bright bass behind you

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:05.893
and I'm just curious.

00:09:05.893 --> 00:09:08.090
I mean, can I convince you to play

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:11.050
just a little something maybe or?

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:11.883
- Yes.

00:09:11.883 --> 00:09:13.860
Well, you know, I don't know
if I have any basses nearby,

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:15.119
but if I did--

00:09:15.119 --> 00:09:17.786
(both laughing)

00:09:18.935 --> 00:09:20.270
- Really?

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.840
- How about blues and A and B flat?

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:24.041
- I love it.

00:09:24.041 --> 00:09:26.624
(guitar music)

00:09:29.679 --> 00:09:32.012
(footsteps)

00:09:35.151 --> 00:09:37.990
Something is moving near.

